<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:data="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Data.Converters;assembly=Avalonia.Base"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="700" d:DesignHeight="596"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.LightingTabView"
             x:DataType="vm:LightingTabViewModel">
  <!--
    The editor's Lighting tab, as design mockup 2f redraws it: THE MODE IS RENDERED ON THE BOARD,
    not named in a dropdown. The board animates the selected effect at its real speed and direction
    while the rail beside it is scrubbed.

    THIS FILE IS THE BOARD COLUMN, AND NOTHING ELSE (issue #128). It used to own the whole screen —
    its own `* | Auto | Auto` columns, its own `GridSplitter`, its own hosting of
    LightingModeRailView, and its own copy of the layer switch. All four moved into
    KeyboardEditorView, for one reason each:

      the columns    the rail is now ONE column of the editor, shared with the key inspector, so
                     that switching tabs cannot move the board by a pixel. Issue #124 had already
                     given the two rails one stored width; two grids meant they still only matched
                     by arithmetic.
      the seam       goes with the column it resizes.
      the rail       is the editor's, drawn beside whichever section is open.
      the switch     is in the tab bar's one switcher slot, beside the profile picker, where the
                     layout layer switch is on the other three tabs. It cost this column a row of
                     height it does not have at the window floor, and it put the editor's switcher
                     in two different places depending on the tab.

    WHAT IS LEFT is the picture, its header, the paint line and the zones — the board and the
    statements about it — laid out in the content column the editor hands this view.

    THE BOARD IS THE SCREEN'S SUBJECT, so it gets the canvas and everything else sits around it:
    its own header naming what it is showing, and the paint line under it. There is no
    `keyboardBoard` surround — the picture draws its own bordered section panels
    (Controls/KeyboardView.axaml), and an inset frame around them was the visible double frame issue
    #91 removed from the Layout canvas.

    WHAT IS *NOT* HERE. No mode name, no parameter, no direction set, no zone and no speed bound is
    spelled in this file: LightingTabViewModel asks Core for every one of them
    (LightingModeParameters, LightingAvailability, LightingZoneCatalog, LayerLightingState). The
    frame timer that drives the animation is in the code-behind, and it does nothing but hand
    elapsed seconds to AdvancePreview — including the reduce-motion decision, which the view model
    re-reads on every call.
  -->
  <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*">

    <!-- Demo mode: everything is explorable, nothing is written (03 §3.5). -->
    <Border Grid.Row="0"
            Classes="card"
            Margin="0,0,0,10"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasStatusMessage}">
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" Classes="body muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
    </Border>

    <!-- The picture, centred as one block with the line that names it. `BoardHeader` is the whole
         point of this screen: "Wave · live preview" while it animates, "Wave · preview" while
         reduce-motion holds it on the effect's first frame. Sans — the mode's name is the app's own
         caption for it, not a token out of the file.

         The caps draw the LIT face (ShowsLighting) and NOT the state badges: the remap bar, the
         macro dot, the tap-and-hold corner and the advisory bar all report the *layout*, and this
         board cannot edit one. The two tabs render the same layer and the same cap view models;
         only what each picture draws differs.

         CENTRED, LIKE THE KEYS BOARD — and the argument this reverses is recorded rather than
         deleted, because it was right when it was written. This block used to be anchored to the
         TOP of the column with a 20 px inset, on a frame's finding: the paint line appeared and
         disappeared with the mode, so every time the rail's selection crossed between Wave and
         Freestyle the row under the picture came or went and slid a centred board about 70 px.
         Top-anchoring made the board's position independent of what was drawn beneath it.

         THAT CAUSE IS GONE. The paint line and the zones are on screen in EVERY mode now (see the
         two blocks below, and LightingTabTests.ThePaintLine_IsOnScreen_WhateverTheModeIs), so
         nothing under the picture changes height as the rail is scrubbed and the anchoring was
         outliving the defect it was added for. Issue #124 chose parity with the Keys board instead
         — the same centred block in a column with no gutter — because switching tabs and finding
         the same board in a different place, at a different size, is what a user actually notices.

         WHAT THE REVERSAL DOES *NOT* TOUCH is the row structure beneath, and the distinction
         matters: issue #123 replaced this block's StackPanel with the Grid below to stop the
         picture overflowing its slot, and the Keys board it is now matching is itself a
         `RowDefinitions="*,Auto"` Grid that is ALSO `VerticalAlignment="Center"`. Anchoring and
         fitting are independent — the star row still hands the board the height that is actually
         left, and centring only decides where the finished block sits in the column. Turning this
         back into a centred StackPanel would reintroduce #123's defect *and* fail the rule it left
         behind.

         A GRID, NOT A StackPanel (issue #123). A vertically-oriented StackPanel measures its
         children with double.PositiveInfinity down its own axis, so the picture's BoardScaleHost
         took its scale from the width alone and the block overflowed the slot — painted over its
         neighbours rather than cut, because nothing on the board chain clips. The board sits in the
         one star row and is therefore fitted to the height that is left after the header and the
         two rows beneath it. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1"
          HorizontalAlignment="Center"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          IsVisible="{Binding Board, Converter={x:Static data:ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
      <!-- The board's row carries a FLOOR, and the long form is what it takes to say so. The three
           Auto rows are measured before the star one and take everything they ask for, and on this
           screen they ask for a lot: at the 720x480 minimum the header, the paint line and the
           wrapped zone buttons want most of the column, which left the picture a few pixels tall.
           Below HeightBoardMin the board stops giving way and the zone row - the bottom-most and
           least urgent thing on the screen - runs past the column instead. -->
      <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="{StaticResource HeightBoardMin}" />
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
      </Grid.RowDefinitions>

      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0"
                 Text="{Binding BoardHeader}"
                 Classes="cardTitle"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                 Margin="0,0,0,10" />

      <controls:KeyboardView Grid.Row="1"
                             DataContext="{Binding Board}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                             ShowsLighting="True"
                             ShowsStateBadges="False"
                             KeySelectedCommand="{Binding $parent[views:LightingTabView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectKeyCommand}" />

      <!-- ===== The paint line ==============================================================
           2f's "Paint · 2 keys selected" with "Select all" and "Clear". It sits directly under the
           picture, exactly as the Layout tab's legend row does and for the same reason: it is a
           statement ABOUT the board, so it is the board's own width and its ends line up with the
           board's ends.

           IT IS ON SCREEN WHATEVER THE MODE IS, AND INERT IN MOST OF THEM (issue #135). The row
           stays — mockup 2f draws it on a board running WAVE, and a row that came and went with the
           mode would take the controls off the one screen the design chose to illustrate them on —
           but its two buttons are DISABLED wherever the mode has no per-key colour, which is every
           mode but Freestyle, Breathe and Frozen Wave, and clicking a cap there does nothing.

           THAT REVERSES 2f's OWN READING, deliberately. The painted colours do belong to the layer
           rather than to the effect over them — "the colors are still on file" — and the 40% paint
           layer still shows them under a mode that will not use them. What changed is whether they
           can be EDITED there: the firmware reads no per-key colour line in Wave (§2.2 writes
           none), so offering a selection was offering a write the board would never honour. The
           caption beside the buttons still reports the selection, which is empty in those modes.

           Whether this board can paint AT ALL is still settled by the tab existing
           (LightingTabViewModel.IsSupported). The mode answers a different question — whether the
           paint is editable right now — and that is LightingTabViewModel.CanSelectKeys.

           `Clear` EMPTIES THE SELECTION (issue #131) — it is the other half of `Select all`, which
           is the button it stands beside. It used to paint the selected keys black, and that read
           as a dead control: with nothing selected, over keys carrying no colour, or under a mode
           whose paint layer is drawn at 0 %, pressing it changed not one pixel. Painting a
           selection off is still a black pick in the picker, and erasing the layer is `Reset All`.

           The swatch rule beside the caption still matters for both: an unlit key is hatched,
           never black. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="2"
            ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto"
            ColumnSpacing="8"
            Margin="0,14,0,0">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                   Text="{Binding Selection.Caption}"
                   Classes="body"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                   Text="{x:Static views:LightingTabView.HatchRule}"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Margin="8,0,0,0" />
        <controls:AccessibleButton Grid.Column="2"
                                   Classes="secondary"
                                   Content="{x:Static vm:LightingPaintSelection.SelectAllCaption}"
                                   Command="{Binding SelectAllKeysCommand}" />
        <controls:AccessibleButton Grid.Column="3"
                                   Classes="secondary"
                                   Content="{x:Static vm:LightingPaintSelection.ClearCaption}"
                                   Command="{Binding ClearSelectionCommand}" />
      </Grid>

      <!-- ===== The zones ===================================================================
           §4's named colour zones and the "Reset All" that erases every per-key colour: the bulk
           versions of the paint line above, so they sit with it rather than at the far end of the
           column — and on screen under the same rule, for the same reason. The whole block is one
           fixed set of chips whatever the mode is, which is what lets the block above be centred
           without the board moving as the rail is scrubbed.

           THEY ARE NOT FREE, and the note that used to say they were was written when the board was
           measured against infinite height. This row is an Auto row above a star one, so every line
           the zone buttons wrap onto is a line the picture does not get; at the 720x480 minimum
           that is what takes the board down to its HeightBoardMin floor. A field that appears or
           vanishes with the mode still moves only itself — the block above is one fixed set of rows
           — but one that WRAPS now resizes the board with it.

           A ZONE CHIP IS A PLAIN BUTTON, NOT A TOGGLE (issue #131). `SelectZoneCommand` adds the
           zone's keys to the paint selection or subtracts them, and the chip shows NO state for it.

           It carried a selected face between #124 and #131, bound to `IsSelected` — "every key of
           this zone is currently selected", pushed in from the selection so that it followed a cap
           click and `Select all` too. The zones OVERLAP (`All` contains everything;
           `Left Module ⊃ Game ⊃ WASD`), so that face was a claim about a set another button could
           move: pressing `Game` and then `WASD` and then `WASD` again removed four keys and un-lit
           `Game` while twenty-five of its keys stayed selected, and after `Select all` every chip
           was lit, so the next press on one of them added instead of removing. A latch that cannot
           be honest is not shown at all — the keys on the board are where the selection is read.

           The `zoneButton` bridge (Styles/Buttons.axaml -> the `ToggleSegment` control theme) stays:
           what is used of it is the neutral chip face and its hover/press ramp, and the theme's
           `.selected` state is simply never entered here.

           THE CHIPS ARE DISABLED IN A MODE WITH NO PER-KEY COLOUR (issue #135), through
           SelectZoneCommand's own CanExecute — a zone press is a selection, so it is gated with the
           rest of them. `Reset All` IS NOT, deliberately: it erases the per-key colours the layer
           carries on file, which exist and are worth erasing whatever effect is running over them.
           It is a file action, not a selection one. -->


      <Grid Grid.Row="3"
            ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto"
            ColumnSpacing="8"
            Margin="0,18,0,0">
        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Zones}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <WrapPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingZoneViewModel">
              <controls:AccessibleButton Classes="zoneButton"
                                         Content="{Binding Caption}"
                                         Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingTabView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectZoneCommand}"
                                         CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

        <controls:AccessibleButton Grid.Column="1"
                                   Classes="secondary"
                                   Content="{x:Static vm:LightingTabViewModel.ResetAllCaption}"
                                   Command="{Binding ResetAllCommand}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Top" />
      </Grid>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
